<Read title="The history of astrology, from Babylon to horoscope apps — Svapna" description="A timeline of astrology over 4,000 years, with separate sequences for India, China and Mesoamerica: Babylonian omens, the first horoscope in 410 BCE, the Greek system, India, Baghdad, the universities of Europe, the newspaper column of 1930, and the apps." path="/stars/history" image="/og/stars.jpg" crumbs={[{ name: 'The sky', path: '/stars' }, { name: 'The history', path: '/stars/history' }]}>
  <p class="small crumb"><a href="/">Svapna</a> · <a href="/stars">The sky</a> · The history</p>
  <h1>Four thousand years, <em>in order.</em></h1>
  <p class="lede">Astrology began as a service to kings, became a science taught in universities, lost that standing in the seventeenth century, and returned in the twentieth as something to read over breakfast. The main sequence follows the tradition that runs from Babylon through Greece to the Islamic world and Europe. Shorter sequences for <a href="#india">India</a>, <a href="#china">China</a> and <a href="#mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> follow it.</p>
  <StarsNav path="/stars/history" />

  <div class="steps">
    {T.map(([when, what, text]) => <div><b>{when}</b><div><p><strong>{what}.</strong> {text}</p></div></div>)}
  </div>

  <div class="prose">

    <h2 id="india">India, in order</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>c. 1000 BCE onward</b><div><p><strong>The moon’s stations.</strong> The Atharva Veda lists the nakshatras, the star groups the moon passes night by night. They are used to time rituals. A short manual, the Vedanga Jyotisha, gives rules for the calendar. There are no signs, planets-in-signs or birth charts yet.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>2nd–4th century</b><div><p><strong>The Greek chart arrives.</strong> The Yavanajataka sets Greek horoscopy in Sanskrit verse. Many technical terms in Jyotisha are Greek words in Sanskrit dress: <em>hora</em> from <em>hōra</em>, <em>kendra</em> from <em>kentron</em>.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>6th century</b><div><p><strong>Varahamihira.</strong> Working at Ujjain, he writes the Brihat Jataka on birth charts and the Brihat Samhita on omens. The large handbook attributed to the sage Parashara, the most used today, was compiled over the following centuries.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>13th century onward</b><div><p><strong>A second import.</strong> Persian and Arabic astrology enters under the Delhi sultans and the Mughals and is written up in Sanskrit as <em>Tajika</em>. It adds the yearly chart cast for each birthday.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>1957–2001</b><div><p><strong>The modern state.</strong> The national calendar standardises the reckoning. In 2001 the University Grants Commission approves degree courses in Jyotisha, and in 2004 the Supreme Court lets the decision stand.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="china">China, in order</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>c. 1250–1050 BCE</b><div><p><strong>Oracle bones.</strong> Diviners of the Shang kings date their questions by the cycle of sixty days and record eclipses and new stars as signs for the king.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>3rd century BCE</b><div><p><strong>Day books and animals.</strong> Almanacs written on bamboo slips, found in tombs at Shuihudi and Fangmatan, list lucky and unlucky days for marriage, travel and building. They contain the earliest lists of the twelve animals. The theory of five phases takes shape in the same period.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>c. 100 BCE</b><div><p><strong>The sky as a court.</strong> The historian Sima Qian, himself the court astrologer, writes a treatise that maps the stars as a celestial palace with officials and provinces. Every dynastic history after his has a chapter of sky omens.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>8th–10th century</b><div><p><strong>The Four Pillars.</strong> Li Xuzhong, in the Tang dynasty, reads fate from the year, month and day of birth. Xu Ziping, in the tenth century, adds the hour and makes the day the centre of the reading. The method is still called Ziping after him.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>1644</b><div><p><strong>Jesuits at the bureau.</strong> The German Jesuit Adam Schall von Bell is made head of the imperial Astronomical Bureau. European methods now compute the calendar, and the bureau goes on choosing lucky days with it.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>1912–today</b><div><p><strong>After the empire.</strong> The Republic adopts the Western calendar. Official sky omens end with the emperors. The animals, the almanac of lucky days and the Four Pillars carry on in private life across East Asia.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="mesoamerica">Mesoamerica, in order</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div><b>c. 300–200 BCE</b><div><p><strong>The oldest secure date.</strong> A painted wall fragment from San Bartolo in Guatemala carries the day “7 Deer”, the earliest firm evidence of the 260-day count. Carvings in Oaxaca that may be three centuries older are disputed.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>3rd–9th century</b><div><p><strong>The Classic Maya.</strong> Kings date their monuments by the count and time wars and accessions by Venus.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>11th–15th century</b><div><p><strong>The painted books.</strong> The Dresden Codex holds almanacs for the 260 days and tables of Venus and eclipses. Aztec day-readers use similar books to read a newborn’s day and choose the day for its naming.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>1562–1570s</b><div><p><strong>Burned, and written down.</strong> The friar Diego de Landa burns Maya books in Yucatán in 1562; four survive anywhere. In central Mexico Bernardino de Sahagún has Aztec elders dictate the meanings of the days, and records them in order to oppose them.</p></div></div>
      <div><b>Today</b><div><p><strong>Still counted.</strong> K’iche’ and other highland Maya daykeepers keep the same count, day for day.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What changed, and what stayed</h2>
    <p>Three things changed. The subject moved from the state to the person: the first omens were about kings and harvests, and a horoscope today is about one reader’s week. The claim moved from events to character: a medieval astrologer said when a man would die, and a modern one describes what he is like. And the standing moved from science to pastime: Ptolemy and Kepler practised it as part of their profession, and no university science department teaches it now.</p>
    <p>The machinery stayed. The twelve signs, the seven classical planets, the houses and the aspects in a chart drawn today are the ones set down in Egypt about two thousand years ago, and the signs themselves are Babylonian.</p>

